The page contains two of the requested functions. There is no function named `is_ignored`; only `is_ignored_by_top_level_config` and `is_path_ignored` exist (plus a related `is_ignored_by_feature_config`).

**`is_ignored_by_top_level_config`**

This function reads the settings and checks inclusion/ignore rules. Note that this function only handles global files include/ignore — there is no VCS or overrides logic here. The order of checks is:

```rust
fn is_ignored_by_top_level_config(&self, path: &Path) -> bool {
    let settings = self.settings();
    let is_included = settings.as_ref().files.included_files.is_empty()
        || settings.as_ref().files.included_files.matches_path(path);
    !is_included || settings.as_ref().files.ignored_files.matches_path(path)
}
```

The logic: a path is ignored if it is not included, or if it matches the ignore list. As the comment describes, it checks whether a file is ignored in the top-level config `"files.ignore"`/`"files.include"`.

**`is_path_ignored`**

This function applies checks in a specific order: first it exempts the config file, then top-level config, then feature-level config:

```rust
fn is_path_ignored(&self, params: IsPathIgnoredParams) -> Result<bool, WorkspaceError> {
    let path = params.rome_path.as_path();
    // Never ignore Biome's config file regardless `include`/`ignore`
    if path.file_name().and_then(|s| s.to_str()) == Some(BIOME_JSON) {
        return Ok(false);
    }
    // Apply top-level `include`/`ignore`
    if self.is_ignored_by_top_level_config(path) {
        return Ok(true);
    }
    // Apply feature-level `include`/`ignore`
    Ok(self.is_ignored_by_feature_config(path, &params.feature))
}
```

**Regarding the order you asked about:** The code applies checks in this sequence — (1) the Biome config file is always exempt, (2) top-level (global) files include/ignore, then (3) feature-level include/ignore.

